    "created_at": "2020-05-05T02:14:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-05-05T02:14:38Z",
    "body": "As I'm also bugged by this issue I would like to give some details to it as per the \"Proposing new features\" part of the FAQ.\r\nSo the problem is:\r\n- Users (at least a big part of them) are accustomed with the notion of 'default action', like pressing Enter in a text input would accept the contents entered on that page and will take them to the next page. The best examples for this functionality are the wizard style workflow, where one has to go through a set of pages to finish some task.\r\nIn classic programs (on every platform I can think of) the availability of this functionality can be clearly seen by the visual clue (mostly an extra border) applied to the default button. Doing/using this on a webpage is currently really hard. On most pages there is no clear indication whether pressing the Enter key would do something special at all, and if it would do, what would it that be!? \r\n- Use case:\r\nA web-shop checkout task, where one has to pass through several relatively independent pages (cart contents, delivery address, invoice address, finalization, payment, finish).\r\n- Requirements:\r\nThe **user** should have clear clues to see which button (if at all) will be activated if s/he chooses to press Enter instead of clicking/tapping a button. And s/he could rest assured that only that one will be activated.\r\nThe **site developer** should easily specify (and as well signal that to the user) which button will handle the pressing of the Enter key. And do this in a concise manner with sane defaults, even without JavaScript and extra CSS or being forced to use some shady techniques involving shuffling the buttons' orders, hiding some buttons or duplicating some of them in order to comply with the current \"the first submit button in tree order\" rule.\r\n\r\nI hope that I could add a little insight to this, and if I can be of any use I happily answer questions.",
